\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {G.1}{\ignorespaces \emph  {Upper panels}: the radiation that heats a parcel of dust, marked by the black point, arrives from every direction having crossed columns of very different length. The arrows are drawn thicker where less material has been crossed. In a layer almost everything arrives along the normal; at the center of a sphere no direction is favored; a filament is intermediate, short across and long along its axis. \emph  {Lower left}: the effective attenuating column $\Sigma _{\rm  a}$ of Eq.~(\ref {eq:seff}) against the fractional depth along a line of sight through a layer, for total surface densities $\Sigma $ of $10^{21}$, $10^{22}$ and $10^{23}$\,cm$^{-2}$. The dotted curves are the column along the shortest path, to the nearer surface of the layer. \emph  {Lower right}: the dust temperature that follows from $\Sigma _{\rm  a}$ through Eq.~(\ref {eq:law}). The dotted line marks the floor temperature $T_{\rm  f}$.}}{19}{figure.G.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:geometry}{{G.1}{19}{\emph {Upper panels}: the radiation that heats a parcel of dust, marked by the black point, arrives from every direction having crossed columns of very different length. The arrows are drawn thicker where less material has been crossed. In a layer almost everything arrives along the normal; at the center of a sphere no direction is favored; a filament is intermediate, short across and long along its axis. \emph {Lower left}: the effective attenuating column $\Sig _{\rm a}$ of Eq.~(\ref {eq:seff}) against the fractional depth along a line of sight through a layer, for total surface densities $\Sig $ of $10^{21}$, $10^{22}$ and $10^{23}$\,cm$^{-2}$. The dotted curves are the column along the shortest path, to the nearer surface of the layer. \emph {Lower right}: the dust temperature that follows from $\Sig _{\rm a}$ through Eq.~(\ref {eq:law}). The dotted line marks the floor temperature $T_{\rm f}$}{figure.G.1}{}}
